nodecode-cron-plan is a rewrite of Moodtuner997's Moodtuner997/hermes-cron-consolidator:

  https://github.com/Moodtuner997/hermes-cron-consolidator
  commit 52acc397a7eb4ccf809e03afbc021a29f440b28b
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Moodtuner997 (LICENSE)

The report was rewritten in Common Lisp from the original's Python: cronfields.py's five-field schedule
expansion and overlap test, analysis.py's grouping of jobs whose prompts are near-duplicates (its difflib
ratio reimplemented as a longest-common-block recursion), the same-skills-and-destination and
same-script groupings, the union-find that makes one candidate of a group, the keeper rule (most fires,
then oldest), the token ceiling averaged over the window's daily usage, the clock-time collision check
that leaves out pollers, and report.py's Markdown and JSON answers and its argument words. The cell reads
the same files the plugin read, cron/jobs.json and cron/usage_audit.jsonl, and writes nothing. It is a
Nodecode cell rather than a Hermes plugin: the slash command is registered through the cell contract,
the settings live in the config's cron-plan section, and the same plan is one Lisp call,
(cron-plan:plan "report").

Left out, because it is not what the report is about: cron/executions.db is not read, so the per-job
failed-run count that came from SQLite is absent and a failure streak is the number jobs.json records;
the CLI entry point (hermes cron-consolidator) has no counterpart, the cell's /cron-plan and
(cron-plan:plan ...) standing in for it; and the Hermes home is named by the section's home setting
rather than discovered from HERMES_HOME, so one set of arguments serves both a profile and a copied
home the way --profile and --home did.
